<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  “Bumper to bumper” is a sales phrase. The document that matters is the warranty or the service contract, and those are not the same product. The factory warranty is the manufacturer’s promise, limited by time, mileage, and a list of what is covered. A vehicle service contract is something you buy, often from a company that is not the manufacturer, with its own administrator, its own deductible, and its own list of parts that are someone else’s problem. Mixing the two up is how people pay for coverage they already have, or discover they never had the coverage they described to the shop.
</p>

<h2>What the manufacturer already promised</h2>
<p>
  New vehicles are sold with a written warranty. The basic coverage and the powertrain coverage are different clocks. The in-service date, not the model year printed on the site you are reading, starts the clock. Mileage ends it. A car that sat on a lot, or a certified used car with a remaining factory term, has to be read off the warranty booklet and the in-service record, not off a rule of thumb about “three years and 36,000 miles.” Some manufacturers write different terms. The booklet in the glovebox is the contract.
</p>
<p>
  Federal warranty law is the Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act, 15 U.S.C. sections 2301 through 2312. The Federal Trade Commission’s plain-language guide to auto warranties and service contracts is at <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/auto-warranties-and-service-contracts" rel="noopener">consumer.ftc.gov</a>. Magnuson-Moss is not a lemon law and it does not set a nationwide count of repair attempts. It does restrict certain tie-in practices. A manufacturer generally cannot condition the warranty on you using its own branded parts or its own dealer for routine maintenance, unless it provides those parts or that service free of charge. Keep receipts anyway. A denied claim often turns on whether the failure is related to skipped maintenance, and the receipts are how you answer that.
</p>
<p>
  Certified pre-owned coverage is a separate warranty, usually from the manufacturer, with its own term that may start when the original warranty ends or may overlap it. Read the start date. “Certified” on a dealer’s website, without the manufacturer’s program, may mean a checklist the dealer invented. Ask which warranty company is on the hook.
</p>

<h2>What you are buying if you buy more</h2>
<p>
  A service contract should name the obligor, the administrator, the deductible, the labor rate it will pay, whether you can choose the shop, and what happens if the company does not answer the phone. The long version of how these contracts are structured is the <a href="/guides/warranties/extended-warranty-guide/">extended warranty guide</a>. The short version is that “exclusionary” coverage (everything is covered except a named list) and “stated-component” coverage (only the named list is covered) fail in different ways. Wear items, trim, glass, and anything the contract calls a maintenance part are commonly excluded even on the expensive tier.
</p>
<p>
  Price the contract against the repairs it would actually pay on this car. A powertrain-only contract does little for a car whose expensive failures are electronics, air suspension, or a hybrid battery, if those parts are not on the list. A comprehensive contract with a deductible close to the cost of the likely repair is a prepayment plan, not insurance. Our <a href="/guides/repairs/average-repair-costs/">repair-cost ranges</a> are a way to test the deductible, not a prediction that your car will need those jobs.
</p>
<p>
  You can usually buy a service contract later, from someone other than the selling dealer. The finance office is convenient and is not required. If the contract is rolled into the auto loan, you pay interest on it. That belongs in the price comparison. Cancellation rights exist on many of these contracts and are time-limited. Ask for the cancellation clause before you sign, and get the answer on the paper, not from the person who is paid to sell it.
</p>

<h2>Recalls and repeated repairs sit next to the warranty, not inside it</h2>
<p>
  A safety recall is free whether or not any warranty is still in force. Do not buy a service contract to “cover” an open campaign. Check the VIN on the <a href="/recall-checker/">recall checker</a> and schedule the dealer. The authority is NHTSA and 49 U.S.C. Chapter 301, summarized at <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a>.
</p>
<p>
  A defect the manufacturer cannot repair after a reasonable chance is a lemon-law question, and the reasonable chance is defined by your state, not by a national “three tries” slogan. Start with the <a href="/guides/lemon-law/">lemon-law guide</a> and your state’s page in the <a href="/states/">state directory</a>, then confirm the current threshold with the state attorney general. Magnuson-Moss may give a separate path when a written warranty was not honored. The two claims get bundled in advertising. They are different statutes. The federal one is explained in <a href="/guides/lemon-law/federal-lemon-law/">federal lemon law</a>.
</p>

<h2>A used car with no warranty left</h2>
<p>
  Once the factory term is over, every mechanical failure is cash unless you bought a contract that covers it or the seller made a written promise. Some states impose a short implied warranty on dealer used-car sales. Some allow the dealer to disclaim it if the disclaimer is conspicuous. The Buyers Guide on a dealer’s used car is where that disclaimer lives. The FTC’s used-car guidance is at <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/buying-used-car-dealer" rel="noopener">consumer.ftc.gov</a>. “As is” means you should assume the next repair is yours, and you should price the car that way.
</p>
<p>
  An independent inspection before you buy is cheaper than discovering the exclusion list after a failure. The <a href="/guides/how-to-check-car-before-buying/">pre-purchase guide</a> is the process. A service contract purchased on a car that already has the failure is often void for a pre-existing condition. Read that clause. It is the one that does the work.
</p>
<p>
  If you are comparing contracts, compare them on the obligor’s name, the exclusion list, the deductible, the labor rate, and the cancellation terms. A lower monthly price that starts those terms over, or that excludes the system you are worried about, is not the cheaper contract. And if a seller quotes a percentage by which one product “usually” beats another, ask for the document. The comparison that matters is the two contracts in front of you.
</p>

